--------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. SIBLING PARITY
The six arms, far bound as written (grep 'relative_addr < CRIME_RE'):

    TLB_A   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_B            <- NEIGHBOUR's base
    TLB_B   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_C            <- NEIGHBOUR's base
    TLB_C   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_C + 0x200    <- OWN base + literal
    TEX     ra < CRIME_RE_TEX    + 0xe0    <- OWN base + literal
    LIN_A   ra < CRIME_RE_LINEAR_A + 0x80  <- OWN base + literal
    LIN_B   ra < CRIME_RE_LINEAR_B + 0x80  <- OWN base + literal

*** FINDING 1 -- THE ROW'S STATED REASON IS WRONG FOR ONE OF ITS THREE MEMBERS. ***
The ledger note explains all three misses with "the TLB windows are bounded by the NEXT
window's constant, not base+size".  That is TRUE of TLB_A and TLB_B and FALSE of TLB_C,
which is `base + 0x200` -- structurally IDENTICAL to TEX, LIN_A and LIN_B, all three of
which DID get a shrink mutant and DID fire.  So `accept tlbc` was not blocked by window
shape at all; it was simply not built.  The asymmetry is 2 arms, not 3, and one third of
the filed obligation is the same edit that already worked three times.
Recorded as an L4 against the row: the reason given for a gap was wrong, and a wrong
reason gets reused -- a future round would have designed a special mutant shape for a
window that needs the ordinary one.

--------------------------------------------------------------------- 2. CLAIM -> ROW
Claim under audit, from #497/a643e18's de-escalation obligation: "the fix owes a row per
de-fatalised predicate proving legal accesses still land", six windows, six accept rows.
  accept tex / lina / linb ... DISCHARGED, measured kills already in table_final.txt
  accept tlba / tlbb / tlbc ... NOT discharged -- rows exist, no mutant ever reddened them
An accept row is discharged by a MEASURED KILL, not by existing.  Three rows owed.
No OTHER added claim sentence in this round asserts an observable consequence: this round
adds no product code.  N/A for the rest of the check, reason recorded.

------------------------------------------------- 3. GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
  guard reads: relative_addr  (and, inside each arm, len and writeflag)
  body  reads: relative_addr, len, writeflag, idata, d->warned
  d->warned write sites: 7  (grep -c 'd->warned |=' src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c)
  bits: TLBA 1<<0  TLBB 1<<1  TLBC 1<<2  TEX 1<<3  LINA 1<<4  LINB 1<<5  DE 1<<6
All seven distinct today -- so no live collision.  BUT the fields DISAGREE in exactly the
way #498 measured: a one-character bit edit (1<<0 -> 1<<1) makes two arms share a latch,
and the second arm to fire then goes silent while the guard is still wrong.  That is a
clause-(d) mutant available here, and #498's `bit5collide` proves the class is real in
THIS FILE rather than in the abstract.  One mutant owed: `M4 bitcollide`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------- 4. CHANNEL
Per row: the mutant it kills, and the observable that differs under that mutant.

  accept tlba  M1 shrink   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_B  ->  < CRIME_RE_TLB_B - 0x100
               channel: store at 0x11f8 falls to the catch-all.  PRE-fix that is exit(1);
               POST-fix it is the latched MSG_W decline.  Oracle is
               `(MSG_W in raw_text) or exited`, want False -- so it moves on BOTH builds.
  accept tlbb  M2 shrink   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_C  ->  < CRIME_RE_TLB_C - 0x100
               channel: identical, at 0x13f8.
  accept tlbc  M3 shrink   ra < CRIME_RE_TLB_C + 0x200  ->  + 0x100
               channel: identical, at 0x15f8.  Same edit shape as the TEX mutant that
               already fired -- see finding 1.
  (all rows)   M4 bitcollide  SGIRE_W_TLBA_LEN 1<<0 -> 1<<1
               channel: the SECOND of TLB_A/TLB_B to decline goes silent.  NOTE this is
               NOT observable by the accept rows -- they run one access in a fresh emu --
               so it needs a two-decline session or it is vacuous by construction.

*** CHANNEL VERIFIED BY READING, NOT ASSUMED, AND ONE ROW SHAPE WAS REJECTED HERE. ***
The accept oracle is NOT a survival-only test -- that is the #489 shape and it would have
been vacuous post-fix, since a declined access and an accepted one both leave the host
alive.  It additionally counts the catch-all MESSAGE, and each row runs in its own emu so
the `(once)` latch is fresh.  That is what makes these three rows killable at all, and it
was checked by opening the file before any mutant was built.
M4's channel does NOT exist in the accept rows as written; recorded here rather than
discovered after a clean table, which is the whole purpose of this check.

CHECKS: 4 run, 0 skipped.  Findings: 1 L4 (wrong reason on record), 1 mutant whose channel
is absent from the rows it would target.
